course: dictionary
lesson: 11378
title: Abrasion: a graze or a wearing away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An abrasion is a word with two meanings, and the clues tell you which one you need. «Mia fell off her bike and got a nasty abrasion on her knee.» Her skin was rubbed raw, so the abrasion is a graze, the sort you get from a fall.
explain: «Over thousands of years, the waves caused abrasion that made the rocks smooth.» Here nothing is hurt. Sand and water rubbed the rock again and again until its surface wore away. This is the science meaning: a surface being worn down by rubbing.
explain: Ask yourself what is being rubbed. If it is skin, it is a graze. If it is rock, sand, ice or land, it is erosion by rubbing. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: An abrasion is a scrape where the top layer of skin has been rubbed off.
fact: «Clean the abrasion with water, then cover it.» This is the first aid use of the word.
fact: In science, abrasion means the wearing away of a surface by rubbing.
fact: Rivers, waves, glaciers and wind can all cause abrasion, so they wear down land and rock.
fact: Abrasion is a noun, and you can have one abrasion or several abrasions.
exercise: «Mia fell off her bike and got an abrasion on her knee.» What is an abrasion? | A broken bone | A type of cold, wet weather | A tiny tooth | *A scrape where the top layer of skin has been rubbed off
exercise: «Over thousands of years, the waves caused abrasion that made the rocks smooth.» What does abrasion do to the rocks? | Makes them grow taller | Paints them bright colours | *Wears their surface away by rubbing | Freezes them solid
exercise: Which of these would most likely be called an abrasion? | *A grazed elbow from sliding on the playground | A library book | A song played on the radio | A cloud floating over the hill
test: «The wind and sand caused abrasion on the old stone wall.» What is happening to the wall? | The wall is growing taller | The wall is being painted | The wall is being built | *Rubbing is slowly wearing its surface away
test: Nurse Sam says, «Wash the abrasion with water and cover it.» Where is the abrasion? | On a computer screen | Inside a river | In the sky | *On the skin
exam: Which is the best way to deal with a minor abrasion on your arm? | Rub it hard with sand | *Clean it with water and cover it | Leave it dirty and uncovered | Pour hot oil on it
